-- statement:
--   **σ-conditional-expectation substrate (de la Peña–Montgomery-Smith 1995 forward bound, eqs (4)–(6)).** On a product probability space $\mu \otimes \nu$ — the *sample block* $\Omega$ (playing the role of $G_2 = \sigma(X^{(1)},X^{(2)})$) times the *sign block* $B$ (the independent symmetric Rademacher signs $\{\sigma_i\}$) — the conditional expectation, given the sample block, of any Banach-valued function $g$ that depends only on the sign block equals its sign-average: $$\mathbb{E}\big(g(\sigma)\mid G_2\big) = \int g\, d\nu \quad \text{a.e.}$$ Formally: for a strongly measurable, $\nu$-integrable $g : B \to E$ (with $E$ a complete real normed space, e.g. a matrix or real statistic), the conditional expectation of $(\omega,\sigma)\mapsto g(\sigma)$ with respect to the $\sigma$-algebra $\mathrm{comap}\,\mathrm{Prod.fst}$ generated by the sample block, under $\mu.\mathrm{prod}\,\nu$, is almost everywhere the constant $\int g\,d\nu$. This is the measure-theoretic core of the de la Peña–Montgomery-Smith σ-randomization step (arXiv:math/9309211, §4 eqs (4)→(6)): the signs are independent of the X's, so conditioning on $G_2$ integrates them to the uniform average that defines $T_{n,k} = 2^k\,\mathbb{E}(\sum f(Z)\mid G_2)$. It packages Mathlib's `condExp_indep_eq` with product-measure coordinate independence (`indepFun_prod`).
-- source:
--   de la Peña & Montgomery-Smith, Decoupling inequalities for the tail probabilities of multivariate U-statistics, Ann. Probab. 23 (1995) 806-816, arXiv:math/9309211, §4 'Proof of the Upper Bound', eqs (4)-(6); Mathlib MeasureTheory.condExp_indep_eq.

import Mathlib.Probability.ConditionalExpectation
import Mathlib.Probability.Independence.Basic
import Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.Prod

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

theorem dlp_sigma_randomization_condexp_eq_sigma_integral
    {Ω B E : Type*}
    [mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω] [mB : MeasurableSpace B]
    [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E] [CompleteSpace E]
    (μ : Measure Ω) (ν : Measure B)
    [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] [IsProbabilityMeasure ν]
    (g : B → E) (hgm : StronglyMeasurable g) (hgi : Integrable g ν) :
    (μ.prod ν)[fun ab : Ω × B => g ab.2 |
        MeasurableSpace.comap (Prod.fst : Ω × B → Ω) mΩ]
      =ᵐ[μ.prod ν] fun _ => ∫ b, g b ∂ν := by sorry
